.TH BC 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
bc \- arbitrary-precision calculator language
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bc
.RB [ \-ilqsvw ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bc
runs programs in the bc language, which has arbitrary-precision decimal
arithmetic, variables, arrays, functions, and C-like control flow.
It reads each
.I file
in turn, then standard input, and runs each statement as soon as it has
been read.
Results go to standard output and are flushed as the statements run.
.PP
A
.B quit
or
.B halt
statement ends
.B bc
at once, so a script that should not go on to read standard input ends
with one of them.
A
.I file
named
.B \-
is a file of that name, not standard input.
With
.BR \-l ,
.B bc
first loads the math library, described below.
.PP
This is the bc applet of
.BR busybox (1),
which shares its code with
.BR dc (1).
.SS Interactive use
When standard input is a console,
.B bc
prints a banner, unless
.B \-q
is given, and reads lines with the line editor of
.BR sh (1),
without a prompt, completion, or a saved history.
Files named on the command line run first.
An error is reported and
.B bc
goes on with the next line, instead of exiting.
After an error in a
.IR file ,
the rest of that file is skipped, and
.B bc
goes on with the next file or with the console.
But a
.I file
that cannot be opened, or that holds a byte that is not allowed (see
.BR NOTES ),
still ends
.B bc
with status 1.
.PP
Ctrl-C stops a running calculation, prints
.BR "interrupted execution" ,
and returns to the line editor.
While a
.I file
runs, Ctrl-C ends
.B bc
with status 1 instead.
At the line editor, Ctrl-C, or Ctrl-D on an empty line, ends the input.
.PP
When standard input is not a console, the first error ends
.B bc
with status 1.
.SS Messages
Errors and warnings go to standard error, as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
bc: \fIfile\fR:\fIline\fR: \fImessage\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
for a
.IR file ,
and as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
bc: \fImessage\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
for standard input.
A message about syntax may end with
\fBat \(aq\fItext\fB\(aq\fR,
where
.I text
is the rest of the line, from the token at which the parser stopped.
.SS Input
Input is ASCII text; see
.BR NOTES .
Statements are separated by newlines or semicolons.
White space between tokens is ignored.
A comment is
.B /*
up to the next
.BR */ ,
which may span lines and does not nest, or
.B #
up to the end of the line.
A backslash directly before a newline is skipped, so a line can continue,
even in the middle of a number.
A backslash anywhere else outside a string or comment is an error.
.PP
A name starts with a lowercase letter and continues with lowercase
letters, digits, and underscores.
Variables, arrays, and functions have separate name spaces, so
.BR f ,
.BR f[] ,
and
.B f()
are three different things.
A variable or array element that has not been set is 0.
These words are reserved:
.PP
.RS 4
.B auto break continue define else for halt ibase if last
.B length limits obase print quit read return scale sqrt while
.RE
.PP
A number is a string of the digits
.B 0
to
.B 9
and uppercase letters
.B A
to
.BR Z ,
with at most one period.
Lowercase letters are names.
A one-digit number, ignoring leading zeros, has the value of its digit,
whatever
.B ibase
is, so
.B A
is always ten.
In a longer number, a digit not less than
.B ibase
counts as
.B ibase
minus one, so
.B ZZ
is the largest two-digit number in the current base.
A number is converted when the statement containing it runs, using the
.B ibase
in effect then, so the body of a function uses the
.B ibase
at the time of the call.
.PP
A string is text in double quotes.
It may span lines, has no escape sequences, and so cannot contain a
double quote.
A string appears only as a statement or as an item of
.BR print .
.SS Expressions
The operators, from the highest precedence to the lowest:
.TP
.B "++ \-\-"
Add 1 to, or subtract 1 from, a variable, an array element,
.BR ibase ,
.BR obase ,
.BR scale ,
or
.BR last .
The value of a prefix form is the new value, and that of a postfix form
is the old value.
.TP
.B "\- !"
Unary minus and logical not.
Not yields 1 for 0 and 0 for anything else.
They have equal precedence, and both bind tighter than
.BR ^ ,
so
.B \-2^2
is 4.
.TP
.B ^
Power, right associative.
The exponent must be an integer, though a fraction of zeros as in
.B 2.0
is accepted, and it may be negative.
.TP
.B "* / %"
Left associative.
The remainder has the sign of the dividend.
Dividing by zero is an error.
.TP
.B "+ \-"
Left associative.
.TP
.B "= ^= *= /= %= += \-="
Assignment, right associative, yielding the assigned value.
The left side is a variable, an array element,
.BR ibase ,
.BR obase ,
.BR scale ,
or
.BR last .
Assignment binds tighter than comparison, so
.B a = 1 < 2
means
.BR "(a = 1) < 2" .
.TP
.B "== <= >= != < >"
Left associative, yielding 1 or 0.
.TP
.B "&& ||"
Equal precedence, left associative, yielding 1 or 0.
Both operands are always evaluated.
.PP
Parentheses group.
A name followed by
.BI ( arguments )
calls a function, and a name followed by
.BI [ index ]
is an array element.
The index is truncated to an integer, and must be from 0 to 2147483647.
An array grows as needed.
.PP
The special variables are
.B ibase
(the input base, from 2 to 36, initially 10),
.B obase
(the output base, from 2 to 999, initially 10),
.B scale
(the number of digits kept after the point, from 0 to 4294967295,
initially 0), and
.B last
(the value last printed, initially 0).
A period alone, as in
.BR "x = ." ,
means
.BR last .
.PP
The built-in functions are:
.TP
.BI length( x )
The number of significant digits of
.IR x :
leading zeros are not counted, even after the point, but trailing zeros
are, so
.B length(0.050)
is 2.
.B length(0)
is 1.
With an array,
.BI length( a [])
is one more than the highest index used, read or written, and at least 1.
.TP
.BI scale( x )
The number of digits after the point of
.IR x .
.TP
.BI sqrt( x )
The square root of
.IR x ,
with the larger of
.B scale
and
.BI scale( x )
digits after the point.
It is an error when
.I x
is negative.
.TP
.B read()
Reads a line from standard input, evaluates it as an expression using the
current
.BR ibase ,
and yields its value.
When the program itself comes from standard input, the line is the next
line of it.
An empty line, or one that is not an expression, is an error.
.PP
The scale of a result follows POSIX.
For
.B +
and
.BR \- ,
it is the larger of the operand scales.
For
.BR * ,
it is the smaller of the sum of the operand scales and the larger of
.B scale
and the operand scales.
For
.BR / ,
it is
.BR scale .
For
.BR ^ ,
it is
.B scale
when the exponent is negative, and otherwise the smaller of the base's
scale times the exponent and the larger of
.B scale
and the base's scale.
The remainder
.B a % b
is
.BR "a \- (a / b) * b" ,
computed with the larger of
.B scale
plus the scale of
.I b
and the scale of
.IR a .
.SS Statements
.TP
.I expression
Evaluates the expression, and unless it is an assignment, writes its
value and a newline.
A parenthesized assignment, such as
.BR "(x = 5)" ,
is written, and
.B x++
writes the old value.
A call of a
.B void
function writes nothing, unless the function returns a value.
.TP
.BI \(dq string \(dq
Writes the string unchanged, with no newline.
.TP
.BI "print " list
Writes each item of the comma-separated
.IR list ,
a string or an expression, with no newline between or after them.
In a string, these sequences are replaced:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.BR \ea ,
.BR \eb ,
.BR \ef ,
.BR \en ,
.BR \er ,
and
.B \et
by the control characters bell, backspace, form feed, line feed, carriage
return, and tab;
.IP \(bu 2
.B \ee
and
.B \e\e
by one backslash.
.RE
.IP
Any other backslash is written as it is.
.TP
.BI { statements }
Groups statements, which are separated by newlines or semicolons.
.TP
.B if (\fIcondition\fB) \fIstatement\fR [\fBelse \fIstatement\fR]
Runs the first statement when the condition is not zero, and otherwise
the second.
A newline may follow the right parenthesis, and the
.B else
keyword, but not two newlines.
The
.B else
must follow the end of the first statement on the same line, with no
semicolon or newline between, as in
.BR "} else {" .
.TP
.BI "while (" condition ") " statement
Repeats the statement while the condition is not zero.
.TP
.BI "for (" init "; " condition "; " update ") " statement
As in C: runs the
.I init
expression once, then repeats the statement and the
.I update
expression while the
.I condition
is not zero.
Each of the three may be empty, and an empty condition is true.
.TP
.B break
Leaves the innermost
.B while
or
.B for
loop.
.TP
.B continue
Goes on to the next iteration of the innermost loop, running the
.I update
expression of a
.B for
loop.
Outside a loop,
.B continue
makes
.B bc
crash.
.TP
.BI "return (" expression )
Ends a function, with the value of the expression.
.B return
alone gives 0, and the parentheses may be left out.
Allowed only in a function body.
.TP
.B halt
Ends
.B bc
when it runs.
.TP
.B quit
Ends
.B bc
when it is read, even in a branch that never runs.
.TP
.B limits
Writes the limits of this implementation when it is read:
.RS
.EX
BC_BASE_MAX     = 999
BC_DIM_MAX      = 2147483647
BC_SCALE_MAX    = 4294967295
BC_STRING_MAX   = 4294967294
MAX Exponent    = 999999999
Number of vars  = 999999999
.EE
.RE
.SS Functions
A top-level statement defines a function:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
define name(parameters) {
    auto locals
    statements
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Each parameter is a name for a value,
.IB a []
for an array that is copied when the function is called, or
.BI * a []
for a reference to the caller's array.
An
.B auto
list, which must come first in the body, names local variables and
arrays, which start out as 0.
Variables are dynamically scoped: a function sees the locals and
parameters of its callers.
A function that ends without a
.B return
gives 0.
.PP
A function may be called before it is defined, but must exist when the
call runs, and the call must have as many arguments as the function has
parameters.
An array is passed as
.IB a [].
Defining a function again replaces it.
The body must be a braced block; the opening brace may be on the next
line.
.PP
.BI "define void " name ()
defines a function that gives no value, so that a call used as a
statement writes nothing, unless a
.B return
in it gives a value.
Used in an expression, such a call counts as 0.
.B void
is otherwise not a keyword.
.SS Output
A number is written in base
.BR obase .
Zero is
.BR 0 ,
a negative number starts with a minus sign, and a number below 1 starts
with the point, as in
.BR .5 .
With an
.B obase
of 16 or less, digits are
.B 0
to
.BR 9 ,
then
.B A
to
.BR F .
With a larger one, each digit is a decimal number padded with zeros to
the width of
.B obase
minus one and preceded by a space, or by the point for the first digit
after it, so 255.5 is
.B " 02 55.50"
with an
.B obase
of 100.
The digits after the point in another base are produced until they cover
the scale of the number, so in base 2,
.B .5
prints as
.BR .1000 .
.PP
A number that does not fit on a line is broken with a backslash and a
newline.
Each line, backslash included, then has one character fewer than the
line length, 69 by default.
The count includes whatever
.B print
wrote earlier on the line, and a number that starts past the limit is
not broken at all.
See
.BR BC_LINE_LENGTH .
.SS Math library
.B \-l
sets
.B scale
to 20 and defines these functions, which compute to the current
.BR scale ,
with angles in radians:
.TP
.BI s( x )
Sine.
.TP
.BI c( x )
Cosine.
.TP
.BI a( x )
Arctangent.
.TP
.BI l( x )
Natural logarithm.
A zero or negative argument gives 1 minus 10 raised to
.BR scale .
.TP
.BI e( x )
The exponential function, e to the power
.IR x .
.TP
.BI j( n , x )
The Bessel function of integer order
.IR n ,
which is truncated to an integer.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-lq ,
and may follow the
.I file
operands.
Long options start with
.B \-\-
and take no argument; a unique prefix of one is enough.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
A first argument of
.B \-\-help
prints a usage message on standard output and exits with status 0.
There is no
.BR \-h .
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Do not print the banner of an interactive session.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-mathlib
Load the math library, which sets
.B scale
to 20.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-standard
Treat the following extensions to POSIX bc as errors:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
A name longer than one character.
.IP \(bu 2
The keywords
.BR continue ,
.BR else ,
.BR halt ,
.BR last ,
.BR limits ,
.BR print ,
and
.BR read ,
and a period for
.BR last .
.IP \(bu 2
The
.B #
comment, and the operators
.BR ! ,
.BR && ,
and
.BR || .
.IP \(bu 2
A comparison outside the condition of
.BR if ,
.BR while ,
or
.BR for ,
or more than one comparison in a condition.
.IP \(bu 2
An empty expression in
.BR for .
.IP \(bu 2
A
.B return
expression without parentheses.
.IP \(bu 2
A function's left brace on a line after the
.B define
line.
.IP \(bu 2
A reference parameter.
.RE
.IP
Each message begins with
.BR POSIX ,
as in
.BR "POSIX does not allow the \(aqprint\(aq keyword" .
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-warn
Write the messages of
.B \-s
as warnings, and go on.
With both options,
.B \-s
wins.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive
Accepted and ignored.
Interactive use depends only on standard input being a console.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version on standard output, and exit with status 0.
The output is three lines, the first of which is
.B bc
and the busybox version.
It is also the banner of an interactive session.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the
.I file
operands are expanded, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
.TP
.B BC_ENV_ARGS
Options and file operands, separated by white space with no quoting,
handled before the command line.
The files are read first.
Only the files and
.B \-v
have an effect; see
.BR NOTES .
.TP
.B BC_LINE_LENGTH
The line length for numbers, as a decimal number.
A line, including its trailing backslash, has at most one character less
than this.
The default is 70, and
.B 0
never breaks lines.
A value that is not a number, is negative, or is 1 or 2 means the default.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When set, has the effect of
.BR \-s ,
and the first
.I file
operand ends the options, so that later arguments such as
.B \-q
are file names.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output and
.B COLUMNS
for the width of the line editor, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
End of input, or a
.B quit
or
.B halt
statement.
In an interactive session, errors in the input do not change this.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option, a
.I file
that cannot be opened or holds a byte that is not allowed, an error in
the input when standard input is not a console, Ctrl-C while a
.I file
runs, or an error reading or writing.
.SH EXAMPLES
Calculate with large numbers:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ echo \(aq2^100\(aq | bc
1267650600228229401496703205376
.EE
.RE
.PP
Divide to 20 digits:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ echo \(aqscale=20; 1/3\(aq | bc
\&.33333333333333333333
.EE
.RE
.PP
Compute pi with the math library:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ echo \(aq4*a(1)\(aq | bc \-l
3.14159265358979323844
.EE
.RE
.PP
Convert between bases:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ echo \(aqobase=16; 255\(aq | bc
FF
$ echo \(aqibase=16; FF\(aq | bc
255
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run a program with a function:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ bc <<\(aqEOF\(aq
define f(n) {
    if (n < 2) return (1)
    return (n * f(n \- 1))
}
f(20)
EOF
2432902008176640000
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write several items on one line:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ echo \(aqfor (i = 1; i <= 3; i++) print i, " ", i^2, "\en"\(aq | bc
1 1
2 4
3 9
.EE
.RE
.PP
Keep a number from being broken across lines:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ echo \(aq2^300\(aq | BC_LINE_LENGTH=0 bc
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Input must be ASCII text.
NUL bytes are ignored.
Any other control character except tab, carriage return, and newline,
including form feed and Ctrl-Z, and any byte from 0x7f up, such as UTF-8
text or a byte order mark, is rejected as
.BI "illegal character 0x" NN
even in a comment or a string.
The whole line is rejected before any of its statements run.
.PP
Files and standard input are read in binary mode, so scripts with LF or
CRLF line ends both work, since a carriage return is white space.
But a backslash must be followed directly by the LF, so a continued line
in a CRLF script is an error.
A carriage return in a string stays in the output, and Ctrl-Z does not
end a file.
Output lines end with LF.
.PP
File names may use
.B /
or
.B \e
and drive letters.
Standard input is read after the last file and cannot be named.
.PP
The error for
.B ibase
says the range is 2 to 16, but values up to 36 are accepted.
.PP
Only the file operands and
.B \-v
of
.B BC_ENV_ARGS
take effect.
Parsing the command line replaces the options it set, so
.B \-l
and the others are lost.
An unrecognized option in it makes
.B bc
crash.
.PP
Ctrl-C is handled by
.B bc
only when standard input is a console.
Otherwise it ends
.B bc
as it does any program.
.PP
Numbers are stored one digit per byte, so large scales and exponents use
a lot of memory.
.PP
Differences from GNU bc:
.IP \(bu 2
A period is a synonym of
.BR last ,
as in BSD bc.
.IP \(bu 2
.B length
accepts an array, as in
\fBlength(\fIa\fB[])\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
.B !\&
has the precedence of unary minus.
.IP \(bu 2
.B read()
accepts an expression, not only a number.
.IP \(bu 2
.B &&
and
.B ||
have equal precedence.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR dc (1),
.BR sh (1)
